[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: The one-node-per-file layout has no executable guard

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SDOC-LAYOUT-CHECK
TITLE: The one-node-per-file layout has no executable guard
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
A check over docs/**/*.sdoc and **/.sdoc/*.sdoc asserting three things per
file: exactly one node tag, a basename equal to the node's UID lowercased plus
.sdoc, and a document TITLE equal to the node's TITLE. Wired into flake.nix
beside strictdoc-cycle-check and strictdoc-fp-check, so it runs in nix flake
check.

None of the three is enforced by anything today. StrictDoc parses a
multi-node document, a mismatched filename and a divergent document title
without complaint, so the convention holds only for as long as every session
remembers it.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
INV-GUARDS-MUST-EXECUTE is the reason this is a node rather than a paragraph in
the skill. A layout rule that only prose enforces decays the first time a
session writes a node the way it wrote them before the carve, and the failure is
silent: the graph still exports, the edges still resolve, and only the context
cost goes back up.

Cheap to write because the carve already proved the predicate. The verification
pass over all 161 files compared basename to UID and document TITLE to node
TITLE and found zero exceptions, so the check starts green and its first red is
a real regression.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Filed 2026-08-27 by the carve session. Not built in that session because adding
a flake check is implementation work and the carve was a migration.

The natural home is checks/strictdoc-layout-check.nix, alongside the eight
existing strictdoc-* checks. Note that a runCommandLocal derivation sets
allowSubstitutes = false and would re-execute on every flake check rather than
being fetched from the cache.
<<<
